{
 "text": "Nora has made thirty-five payments on her old loan, one a month since she moved in. With each one, a little more goes to paying down what she owes. A refinance starts a fresh thirty-year clock, and in the early years of any mortgage, most of each payment goes to interest. So the new loan pays down her balance more slowly than the old one would have. By month twenty-four, she owes one thousand one hundred thirty-three dollars more than if she had kept the old loan.\n\n[thoughtful] If she sold the house then, that one thousand one hundred thirty-three dollars would come out of her sale money, so it cancels part of what she has saved. Her savings need a few more months to cover it, and the gap keeps growing a little while they do. [serious] Count it, and break-even moves from month twenty-four to month thirty.",
